5
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
 If you're building an app, there's one very simple question you need to ask yourself before anything else.

6
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:52,000
 Who is this app for, and what do they actually need it for? That question is the heart of UX research, and it's a big part of what I study in my master's degree in human computer interaction.

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:02,000
 In this video, I'm going to start from the scratch, from the beginning, UI, UX, and then we'll walk it all the way home to UX research, how it is done, where UX research is, and all of that.

8
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 You've probably heard people say UI, UX, like one word, UI, UX designer, UI, UX course, but UI and UX are two different things.

9
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 UI stands for user interface. It is what you can see and touch, the button, the colors, the layout, the icons, the screen.

10
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:31,000
 UX, on the other hand, stands for user experience. It is how the old product feels to use. Can a user find what they're looking for? Does it make sense to the user? Did the users get frustrated using it? Did the user come back to use it?

11
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:40,000
 So you can have a beautiful and half with a beautiful interface that is a nightmare to use. In that case, we have a great UI, but terrible UX.

12
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 So your UI is kind of part of your UX. The experience is just much more bigger than what is on the screen.

13
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 Now, what is UX? User experience, or UX, covers everything about how a person experiences a product or a service. The time was popularized, subscribe to that.

14
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:12,000
 The time was popularized by Don Norman back in the 80s when it was at Apple. And the way he and Jacob Nielsen describe it is that user experience cover every part of a person's interaction with a company, its services and its products.

15
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:25,000
 And all of this connects back to an idea called human centered design. The idea is simple. You can make sense of it in, I love this phrase that I found on like a media article, it says that good design doesn't start with screens, it starts with people.

16
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:33,000
 So you start from the people who use a product, their needs, their abilities, their limitations, their real lives, and you design around them.

17
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:51,000
 So now, we know what UX is. Now what is UX research? UX research is how you get to know your users. It is the work of understanding the people who would use a product, what they need, where they struggle, what they prioritize, so that you can view the product the right way and build it in a way that actually works for them.

18
00:02:51,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 Why aren't we doing this old research? The idea is simple. You don't want to build the wrong thing. You could choose to not do it and guess your way through, but you don't want to build the wrong thing.

19
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:07,000
 Building is very expensive. Even with AI, you used to spend your time and your tokens, and in some cases, you probably still need a thing.

20
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 A lot of products fail because the teams build something that maybe nobody actually needs. Some other products fail because they build the right thing, but they build it in a way that people just couldn't find their way around it, or people just couldn't figure it out.

21
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 UX research helps to catch both problems helly on. It is cheaper to change your idea or change your sketch than it is to rebuild and have that is already life.

22
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:39,000
 And doing UX research means that decisions about the products come from what the users, the real people, actually do. Otherwise, they just come from a random person on the team.

23
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:49,000
 Now, who is a UX researcher? Well, I mean, a UX researcher does UX research. A UX researcher works closely with designers, product managers, engineers, and the people running the business.

24
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
 Their job is to bring real users into those conversations.

25
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:04,000
 A typical way for a UX researcher might look like planning a study, recruiting people to take part, running user interviews or usability testing, then sitting down with the notes or the recordings to find patterns.

26
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:13,000
 And finally, sharing what they found from these users or about these users with the team in a way that actually changes what gets built.

27
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 People usually mix who a UX researcher is and who a UX designer is a lot. A UX researcher's job is to understand the users, what they need.

28
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
 A UX designer's job is to take that understanding and design the product around it.

29
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:34,000
 In smaller teams, there's usually just one person that ends up doing both jobs. And there are also a lot of product managers that do research as part of their jobs as well.

30
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:46,000
 What you need to dive into UX research is just genuine curiosity about users, about people. You have to be really good at taking the time to study people, find patterns, and just be really good at listening.

31
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:53,000
 Basically, you must be interested in people. When I started reading and studying about UX research, I kept running into different ways that people categorize it.

32
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
 You go on Coursera. Coursera has quite a number. You go to different places. It's a different kind of categorization everywhere.

33
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:11,000
 You see quantitative and... describe that. You see generative and evaluative, qualitative and quantitative, behavioral and attitude in our primary and secondary, remote, in-person, moderated, unmoderated.

34
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:18,000
 But the useful way to think about it is that some of these peers describe what kind of information you get, like quantitative and qualitative.

35
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,000
 And others describe how you run the research, like remote or in-person.

36
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:32,000
 Anyways, there's a lot of categorization. I will focus on the first three big peers in this video. Generative and evaluative, qualitative and quantitative, behavioral and attitude in our...

37
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:40,000
 The first big way to split UX research is generative versus evaluative. Generative UX research happens early, before anything is designed.

38
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:47,000
 It asks questions like, "What is the real problem? What do people need? How do they get by right now without this thing that we are about to build?"

39
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:57,000
 It generates ideas for what to build. For example, for my thesis, I'm doing generative research. Evaluative research, on the other hand, happens later, after there is a design.

40
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:04,000
 It asks questions like, "Does this product actually work? Can users use it? Where do the users get stuck while using it?"

41
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
 So you are evaluating something that already exists.

42
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 So generative UX research helps you decide what to build. And evaluative UX research tells you whether what you build works.

43
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:23,000
 The second way to split UX research is qualitative versus quantitative. Quantitative UX research is about numbers.

44
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:31,000
 How many people completed the task? What percentage dropped off at checkout? How long did it take? It tells you what is happening and how much of it.

45
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:38,000
 Qualitative UX research is about understanding. Why did the users drop off? What were they thinking? What confused them?

46
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:49,000
 It's usually smaller and more in depth. It tells you the why. Usually, you want both. You want the numbers to show where the problem is and you want the conversation to show why the problem is happening.

47
00:06:49,000 --> 00:07:01,000
 Now, the big way to split UX research is behavioral versus attitudinal. Attitudinal UX research is about what users say, what they think, what they feel, what they tell you that they do.

48
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:08,000
 Surveys and interviews are mostly attitudinal. Behavioral UX research, on the other hand, is about what people actually do.

49
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:12,000
 So you watch them use the thing. You look at the data on what they click.

50
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:19,000
 And the reason why I want to amount on this, because this is one of the things that was so admired in my years during my first semester,

51
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:23,000
 that what people say and what people do are often very different.

52
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:29,000
 What people say, what they give you in a survey is usually different from what they do when you watch them.

53
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,000
 If you ask someone how they use an app, they will give you an answer.

54
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
 If you watch them use the app, they could end up doing something completely different from the answer that they gave you.

55
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:43,000
 And they might genuinely believe what they told you. We are human beings. We are just not very good at noticing our own behaviors.

56
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:51,000
 So good UX research watches, as much as it acts, what people say still matters. Sometimes they do exactly what they tell you.

57
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:57,000
 But when what they say and what they do don't match, then working out why they don't match is also a very useful finding.

58
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,000
 Now there are a few more categories of UX research that I'll go over very quickly.

59
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
 The primary research is the research you do yourself, like running your own interview.

60
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:10,000
 Secondary research uses what already exists, like published studies, industry reports and all of that.

61
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,000
 Remote UX research happens over a video call through an online tool.

62
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:19,000
 In-person research happens in the same room where you can notice things like body language and all of that.

63
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:25,000
 And then we have moderated research, where a researcher is there, guiding the session, asking follow-up questions.

64
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:31,000
 And unmoderated research, where people complete tasks on their own, usually through a tool that records them.

65
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:36,000
 So how is UX research actually done? There are a lot of methods that I've actually mentioned in the earlier part.

66
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:42,000
 There's user interview, where you sit down with people, you talk to them about their lives, their work, their frustration, maybe how they use their product and all of that.

67
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:50,000
 There's usability testing. You give someone a task to do with your design, maybe with your app, you watch how they do the tasks, usually while they think out loud.

68
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,000
 Then we have surveys. You can reach a lot of people quickly with surveys.

69
00:08:53,000 --> 00:09:00,000
 But surveys usually have less depth. And then there's contextual inquiry, where you go to where people work and live and watch them in their real environment.

70
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:09,000
 There are also diary studies, where people record their experiences over days or weeks, so you see patterns that maybe a single session would have missed.

71
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:16,000
 There's CAD sorting. There's A/B testing. There's all kinds of UX research methods that we can't go over in this video.

72
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:21,000
 You might be thinking that UX research sounds like something that only big companies with big budgets can do.

73
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
 But even as a small startup, some research is always better than no research.

74
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:33,000
 You don't need a lab. Talk to five people who would actually use your product before you start to build, maybe yourself and a few friends.

75
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:39,000
 Watch three of your friends try your app on a video call with messages, with feedback. That also counts.

76
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:46,000
 Because good design goes so much deeper than just a nice visual. It's about whether the thing actually works for the people using it.

77
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:54,000
 Now, another question I wanted to ask is that is UX research the same as user research?

78
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:00,000
 Most of the time, people use these two times. They mean the same thing. You will see, even in job titles, that use either one.

79
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:09,000
 Now, user research is the broader one. UX research sits under it. User research is about understanding the users themselves, the users that need their habits, their lives.

80
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:18,000
 And it can fit into a lot of decisions that go beyond design. For example, research into how customers feel about your prices or your delivery time is user research.

81
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:26,000
 It isn't really about the experience of your product. UX research is more focused on how people experience a specific product or service.

82
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:31,000
 It always, they share the same goal, which is making sure that decisions are based on real users.

83
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:37,000
 And again, both of them also differ from market research. For example, market research is more about what people would buy.

84
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:43,000
 Right? UX research is about how people use the product, how it makes them feel, how it feels to them as well.

85
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:47,000
 Will AI replace UX research? Very good question that I also wanted to answer.

86
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:52,000
 AI too is already doing a lot of the slow work. They can transcribe interviews, they can summarize hours of notes.

87
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:58,000
 We save UX research as a lot of time. I cannot even imagine having to do my thesis four years ago.

88
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:03,000
 I probably would do it, but oh my God, it would be a lot of work. So AI is changing how UX research gets done, right?

89
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:10,000
 In a good way, I think, if you use it well. Now, if you're thinking about a career in UX design, I'm still a student.

90
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
 I'm currently doing my master's in human computer interaction.

91
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:19,000
 And from what I've noticed, the number of jobs with the title UX researcher is actually small compared to other design rules.

92
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:25,000
 I think it's because a lot of companies expect their designers and maybe their product managers to just do some research work themselves.

93
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:30,000
 So companies have fewer people whose old job is research. But it's still a very much needed skill.

94
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
 I think it's just the entry level that might be hard to break into.

95
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:39,000
 I wouldn't know yet, honestly. I'm not in the job market for UX research yet, but that much I do know now.

96
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:44,000
 And as far as to get into UX research, the way I'm getting into it is by getting a master's degree.

97
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
 But I want to believe you can get into it via different ways.

98
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,000
 You can learn the basics, the method, when to use one way, how to ask questions.

99
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:57,000
 There are a few guys from places like Nelson Norman Grove, Mays Interaction Design Foundation.

100
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,000
 You learn the book. There are a lot of books as well.

101
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,000
 And also try to do real research, something that nobody is paying you for.

102
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:09,000
 Just pick an app you use, interview a few people, watch them use it, write up what you find, write your first case study or something like that.

103
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:13,000
 You can also volunteer to do research with small business, things like that.

104
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:20,000
 A lot of UX researchers, from what I've noticed, come from other fields, psychology, teaching, marketing, journalism, computer science.

105
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:25,000
 So if your job already involves talking to people, figuring out what they need, maybe this is something you might love.

106
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:33,000
 And of course, degrees going to school, like the way I'm doing, is actually also a very valid route, maybe even the most common one.

107
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:39,000
 So that's UX research. It's a very long video, so if you go but in the middle, I'll skip through a lot of it.

108
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:46,000
 Just don't forget this phrase that I did find it from a medium as if I can, is that good design doesn't start with screens.

109
